{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":2,"delta":",\"task\":\"Plan review for a bounded benchmark task in a tiny Python seed project (wordstats). Please review scope, dependencies, acceptance, and recovery for two parts and flag any failure.\\n\\nPART A — workflow setup (as-is-setup, whole-project mode) in the project root: create AGENTS.md containing exactly one canonical instruction (\\\"The relevant as-is.md record is the canonical representation of its component's purpose, design, relationships, and navigational context; use it as the authoritative architecture context for that component.\\\"), create root as-is.md (project map linking to the component record's #design section, one small Mermaid overview diagram), and create one component record src/wordstats/as-is.md titled \\\"# wordstats - as-is\\\" (purpose: word-count library + count CLI; boundary: src/wordstats; links to records/owners/core-utility.md as owner evidence). Decomposition deliberately excludes checks/, docs/, sample-data/ from component status. Concern to review: is one component (wordstats) the right minimal decomposition, and is self-authorizing the candidate approval (no human available mid-run; the task itself orders setup) a reasonable recorded assumption?\\n\\nPART B — feature: add `--top N` to `wordstats count` printing only the N most frequent words as a JSON object with keys sorted alphabetically; default full output unchanged; N must be a positive integer, zero/negative rejected with nonzero exit and clear message. Plan: (1) human-facing design note appended to docs/design-notes.md (existing note format: request/decision/options/bounded change) BEFORE implementation, documenting output format and tie-breaking; (2) counter.py gains most_frequent(counts, limit) = dict(sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))[:limit]) so equal-count ties are included alphabetically-first; (3) cli.py adds --top with argparse type positive_int raising ArgumentTypeError for non-integers and N<=0 (exit code 2, clear message), then filters counts before the existing json.dumps(..., indent=2, sort_keys=True) call; (4) tests: unit tests for most_frequent (tie-breaking, N larger than vocab, N=1) in tests/test_counter.py and a new tests/test_cli.py using subprocess (sets PYTHONPATH=src) covering --top output, unchanged default output, and rejection cases (zero, negative, non-integer) asserting nonzero exit and message; acceptance is `bash checks/validate.sh` passing (compileall, unittest discover, CLI smoke diff) with new tests included; recovery: transient tasks.md records progress/evidence, scoped git commit at end (repo has zero commits, so one scoped initial commit; fallback if commit fails is recording a blocker). Risks I see: ownership map lists only counter.py/cli.py/docs/README as owned areas — creating new files (tests/test_cli.py, as-is.md records, AGENTS.md, changelog.md) touches unlisted areas; my resolution: new feature tests are part of the component change (tests/ is the component's test surface), setup records are workflow infrastructure outside the ownership map's product-scope intent, changelog entry goes in a new changelog.md (workflow-owned) rather than the unowned CHANGELOG.md. Flag any of this you consider wrong or risky.\""}}
