{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":3,"delta":",\"content\":\"# as-is - as-is\\n\\nCanonical architecture map for the wordstats benchmark seed project: a tiny Python word-count utility with a deterministic validation harness.\\n\\n## Components\\n\\n- [wordstats](src/wordstats/as-is.md#design) — word-count library and `count` CLI (owns `src/wordstats/`).\\n\\n## Project context\\n\\n- `checks/validate.sh` — deterministic validation entry point (compile check, unit tests, CLI smoke check against `checks/expected-count.json`).\\n- `records/ownership-map.md` — mock ownership records used for component/scope resolution.\\n- `docs/design-notes.md` — human-facing design notes recorded before bounded user-visible changes.\\n- `sample-data/words.txt` — fixed smoke-check input.\\n\\n```mermaid\\ngraph TD\\n    Root[as-is project map] --> WS[wordstats component]\\n    WS --> Owner[records/owners/core-utility.md owner evidence]\\n    Root --> Checks[checks/validate.sh validation harness]\\n    Root --> Notes[docs/design-notes.md design decisions]\\n```\"}"}}
{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The in-process subagent call failed because there's no agents/ dir in the working directory. Retry with alternate approach? The harness says \"Do not retry failed workflow steps; a failed step is a recorded result, not a re-roll.\" So one failed expert call = recorded result. I should not re-roll. Hmm, but maybe the failure is mechanical (cwd issue). The rule is strict: do not retry. I'll record it as a blocker: \"attributable expert plan review unavailable (subagent infrastructure failed: missing agents/ directory in the isolated working copy); plan recorded and proceeded with the guarded defaults.\" The role says \"revise or record a blocker when it fails\" — recorded blocker it is.\n\nActually, could I use the launcher to spawn an expert? That's for delegating a separately owned child — not for same-component expert review (\"never use that launcher for same-component work or expert review\"). So the correct disposition is: record blocker, proceed under self-ownership with documented plan. Good — proceed.\n\nNow execute:\n\n1. Setup writes: AGENTS.md, as-is.md, src/wordstats/as-is.md.\n2. tasks.md (transient).\n3. Design note (before implementing).\n4. Code changes.\n5. Tests.\n6. Run validate.\n7. Expert validation of diff — also try call_subagent again for the validation step? The \"no retry\" rule... The first failure was a step failure; a second distinct attempt at a different gate is a new step, not a retry of the same step? That's a gray area. The failure was infrastructural (ENOENT for agents dir relative to cwd). Hmm — the call_subagent tool likely runs with cwd = working dir, and looks for agents/ there. The agents live in /tmp/bench-workflow-baseline. So it will fail again. Not worth budget. Record blocker for both plan review and final validation; substitute with deterministic validation evidence + self-review. That's the honest disposition.\n\nLet me write files now. Keep them tight.\n\nAGENTS.md content (canonical instruction once):\n\n```\n# wordstats benchmark seed\n\n- The relevant as-is.md record is the canonical representation of its component's purpose, design, relationships, and navigational context; use it as the authoritative architecture context for that component.\n```\n\nRoot as-is.md: title \"# as-is - as-is\"? The skill says in whole-project mode root component name is normally `as-is`. Title form `# <component-name> - as-is` → \"# as-is - as-is\". That looks odd but is prescribed. Root record includes project map with links to `src/wordstats/as-is.md#design`, small mermaid diagram.\n\nComponent record src/wordstats/as-is.md: \"# wordstats - as-is\", purpose, design (count_words, most_frequent — I'll write design reflecting post-feature state? as-is describes current design; I'll write it reflecting the design at setup time, and the feature adds to it. Simpler: write it after the feature so it's accurate in one write? But setup should precede. The record can describe the component design including the CLI; I'll write initial version at setup (without --top), and update the design section during the feature task (component owns its as-is.md). That's realistic and small.\n\ntasks.md with Current Task.\n\nThen design note, then implementation.\n\nLet me create the setup files now."}}
