ent-builder) prescribes the `as-is-setup` skill for adopting the workflow in an existing project. That skill requires: setup plan, root `as-is.md`, component records, canonical instruction in AGENTS.md, validation.\\n\\nBut also the role says I'm the as-is component-builder building a bounded component from its `as-is.md`... The task is: first setup (as-is-setup), then implement feature following implementing-component-tasks presumably.\\n\\nBudget: $2 total including any subagents. I should avoid delegating to subagents (cost). The role requires \\\"attributable expert plan review before edits\\\" and \\\"expert validation\\\" — via call_subagent. Each call costs tokens. With a cheap flash model, a couple of small calls is fine. But to stay safe on budget and wall clock, I'll do minimal, bounded calls: one plan review and one final validation via call_subagent (role \\\"expert\\\"?). Let me check what roles exist under agents/. The agent.md mentions agents/<role>/agent.md. Let me check the skills directory for building-components and implementing-component-tasks to follow the procedure. Given budget constraints, I should be pragmatic: read the two most relevant skills (as-is-setup already read; implementing-component-tasks; committing-completed-work maybe not needed since no git commit requirement — the working dir is \\\"not a git repository\\\" per task; there's a .git but no commits. Committing-completed-work requires git; the harness says don't write outside working dir. I can create a commit in the local git repo? The task says \\\"Perform all project work there\\\" — committing locally is fine and it's a scoped durable handoff. But git has no commits and no user config; a commit would need identity. I could set local user.name/email. Hmm, but guard clause: \\\"Do not amend, push, create pull requests, change branches\\\" — commit is allowed as part of the workflow (committing-completed-work). Let me read that skill and implementing-component-tasks quickly.\\n